From c2f45f86ff38be4e98c7cba2071a62d12564f5e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Sedgmen <dsedgmen@redhat.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:02:16 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 04/28] Add since_common.sh: apply the collection window where
 oc cannot

`oc adm must-gather --since` reaches collection in only two ways: as --since
on `oc adm inspect`, and as --since on an `oc adm node-logs` *journal* query.
It reaches nothing else, and the scripts that collect files have had no way
to honour it.

Measured on a 4.18.54 cluster:

* `oc adm node-logs --role=master --path=kube-apiserver` returns a
  md5-identical listing with no --since, --since=-5m, --since=-30m and
  --since='2026-10-16 06:00:00'. 41 entries, 30 rotated audit files, every
  time.
* A rotated audit file returns all 134269 lines in every one of those cases,
  first event 11 hours before the cutoff.
* Querying the kubelet directly is no better: it validates the parameter --
  an RFC3339 'Z' value is rejected with a format error -- but does not apply
  it to plain file serving.

oc attaches the since parameter only when the path is 'journal'
(pkg/cli/admin/node/logs.go); the flag help says "Only applies to node
service logs". So this is not something a script can fix by passing the
arguments more carefully. It has to do the filtering itself.

Two shapes of collection need two primitives:

  filter_names_since     for collection that produces a listing whose entries
                         carry a timestamp. Entries with no recognisable
                         timestamp always pass through, which is load-bearing:
                         it keeps the live audit.log, termination.log, .lock
                         and .terminating, the files most likely to hold
                         recent events.

  since_find_predicate   for collection that tars or copies a whole directory
                         and has no notion of time at all. Expands to
                         '-newermt @<cutoff>', or to nothing without a window,
                         so the unbounded command is unchanged.
                         copy_pod_dir_since builds on it to replace an
                         `oc cp <pod>:<dir>`, which cannot express a bound,
                         with the find|tar stream gather_network_logs already
                         uses -- so out-of-window files never cross the wire.

trim_jsonl_since makes the audit window exact rather than merely
file-granular by trimming the file that straddles the cutoff. It is
deliberately tolerant: a line that is not JSON is passed through verbatim,
because /var/log/etcd is plain text, and so is a JSON object without the
timestamp field. Only records that parse, carry the field and predate the
cutoff are dropped, so it cannot silently empty a file it did not understand.

Everything is a no-op without a window, so sourcing this cannot change a
script's default behaviour. 30 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
